
====================================================================================================
S1. Reproduce the corpus's OWN frozen MG number, to prove the machinery matches
====================================================================================================
  If Milgrom's theta is set to 1 -- i.e. mu(a_ex/a0), the modified-GRAVITY prescription --
  Eq. (35) must return the pre-registration's frozen framework-as-MG target of 1.137.
  a_ex/a0 = 1.778e-10/9.360e-11 = 1.8996
  gamma_v = 1/sqrt(mu_fw(1.8996)) = 1.1390   vs frozen 1.137 (script asserts |asy - 1.137| < 0.005)
  [OK] reproduced to 0.0020 -- so this script's mu, its gamma_v convention and its frozen a_ex all agree with the banked pre-registration, and any change below is due to theta alone

====================================================================================================
S2. What theta(0) does to the EFE quenching -- Milgrom's own worked case
====================================================================================================
  His sentence: 'for wide binaries in the solar neighborhood we have a_ex/a0 ~ 2, and even a
  value of theta(0) of a few can have a large impact on 1 - mu ... since 1 - mu[2 theta(0)]
  can be rather smaller than 1 - mu(2).'  Quantified on the framework's own mu:
   theta(0) arg = theta*2        mu    1 - mu  vs theta=1
      1.000         2.000    0.7808    0.2192      1.000x
      2.000         4.000    0.8828    0.1172      0.535x
      2.718         5.437    0.9123    0.0877      0.400x
      3.004         6.008    0.9202    0.0798      0.364x
  [OK] 1 - mu falls by 2.7x between theta(0)=1 and theta(0)=3 at a_ex/a0 = 2 -- Milgrom's claim reproduced on the framework's own interpolation, so the MI EFE really does quench harder than the MG one

====================================================================================================
S3. *** DOES IT CLOSE THE PLANETARY LIABILITY? NO, AND IT CANNOT. ***
====================================================================================================
  Eq. (34) puts theta in an ADDITIVE term inside mu's argument:
      A(omega_in) = a_in + theta(omega_ex/omega_in) a_ex
  For a planet, a_in is its own Newtonian acceleration and a_ex the Galaxy's. The anomaly is
      a - a_N = a_N (1/mu - 1) ~ a_N a0 / (2A),  which is a0/2 when A ~ a_N.
  So theta suppresses the anomaly ONLY insofar as theta(0) a_ex competes with a_in:
  planet     a_in (m/s^2)   a_in/a_ex  anomaly, theta=1     theta=3  theta=e^1.1
  Mercury      3.9574e-02    2.23e+08        4.6800e-11  4.6800e-11   4.6800e-11
  Earth        5.9301e-03    3.34e+07        4.6800e-11  4.6800e-11   4.6800e-11
  Mars         2.5542e-03    1.44e+07        4.6800e-11  4.6800e-11   4.6800e-11
  Saturn       6.4579e-05    3.63e+05        4.6800e-11  4.6800e-11   4.6800e-11

  Earth 2-sigma limit on a constant sunward anomaly: 3.660e-14 m/s^2
    theta(0) =  1.000  ->  anomaly 4.6800e-11  =    1278.7x over
    theta(0) =  3.000  ->  anomaly 4.6800e-11  =    1278.7x over
    theta(0) =  3.004  ->  anomaly 4.6800e-11  =    1278.7x over

  theta(0) REQUIRED to bring Earth to the bound: 4.261e+10
  Milgrom's own examples span theta(0) = 2 to 3.00; his general family
  theta(y) = e^((1-y)/q) gives theta(0) = e^(1/q), unbounded as q -> 0, so there is no HARD
  ceiling -- but the requirement is 1.57e+10x his e-example and would need
  q = 1/ln(theta(0)) = 0.0409, i.e. a theta falling by 4.3e+10 between
  y = 1 and y = 0. That is not 'of the order of a few' by any reading.
  [OK] the planetary liability needs theta(0) ~ 4.3e+10, which is 1.4e+10x Milgrom's largest quoted example -- THE FREQUENCY-RATIO ROUTE DOES NOT CLOSE IT. The reason is structural: theta enters additively against a_in, and at Earth a_in/a_ex = 3.3e+07, so the external term is invisible
  AND THE DEEPER REASON IT CANNOT: the a0/2 anomaly is a SINGLE-FREQUENCY effect. Milgrom's
  own result is that the algebraic MOND relation holds exactly for single-frequency
  (circular) trajectories, and a planetary orbit is very nearly one. A construction whose
  content is in frequency RATIOS has no purchase on a trajectory with effectively one
  frequency. The fifth constant omega_c was doing a job that ratios structurally cannot do.

====================================================================================================
S4. *** THE REGIME WHERE theta WOULD PAY -- AND WHY THIS FRAMEWORK CANNOT COLLECT ***
====================================================================================================
  Wide binaries ARE Eq. (35)'s regime: a_ex/a0 ~ 2, and omega_ex << omega_in (the pair orbits
  far faster than the Galaxy), so theta -> theta(0), its maximum. What theta(0) WOULD give:
  a0 footing         g_ext                       a_ex/a0   th=1.00   th=2.00   th=2.72   th=3.00
  canonical rho_DE   primary (McMillan-class)      1.900    1.1390    1.0678    1.0495    1.0447
  canonical rho_DE   alt (Vc^2/R0)                 2.220    1.1182    1.0578    1.0422    1.0382
  alt rho_total      primary (McMillan-class)      1.573    1.1692    1.0823    1.0601    1.0542
  alt rho_total      alt (Vc^2/R0)                 1.839    1.1438    1.0701    1.0512    1.0462

  Taken at face value that would sit at gamma_v 1.0382-1.0823, i.e.
  BELOW the frozen MI point target 1.09 on every theta(0) >= 2, and partly below
  the frozen band's lower edge 1.05. A first pass of this script concluded an
  amendment was therefore owed. THAT CONCLUSION IS WITHDRAWN. It does not apply, because:

  (i) THE KERNEL CANNOT SOURCE theta. mi_theta_efe_from_closure_2026.py S1 shows, sympy-exact,
      that on the oscillatory branch |K| = 1 identically. theta's y-dependence would need |K|
      to vary with frequency; it cannot. Every bound orbit sits deep inside that regime.
  (ii) THEOREM B FORCES QUADRATURE, NOT LINEAR ADDITION. <Box_u>_u = +|a|^2 exactly on every
      timelike worldline, so the closure's argument is |a_in + a_ex|^2 -- the external field
      enters in quadrature WITH A VECTOR CROSS TERM. Milgrom's Eq. (34) is the linear
      a_in + theta a_ex with a scalar multiplier. Different structures; the framework is
      pinned to the first.
  Both were already committed BEFORE this script ran, and Amendment 2 to the DR4
  pre-registration already states it verbatim: 'no multiplying phase function
  theta(omega_ex/omega_in) is available'. Amendment 2 was right.
  [OK] the numbers themselves are not in dispute -- theta(0) >= 2 WOULD give gamma_v 1.0382-1.0823, below the frozen point target 1.09. What is in dispute is whether this framework may use them, and it may not

====================================================================================================
S5. So the frozen target STANDS, and MI-vs-MG stays undecidable HERE
====================================================================================================
  Frozen DR4 error model: sigma(gamma_v) = 0.0191 at N = 30,000 pairs.
  If theta were available, MG (theta=1, 1.1390) and MI would separate by:
   theta(0)  gamma_v MI  separation   sigma
      2.000      1.0678      0.0712    3.7s
      2.718      1.0495      0.0895    4.7s
      3.004      1.0447      0.0943    4.9s
  -> 3.7-4.9 sigma. That decidability is REAL, and it belongs to
  Milgrom's general MI models. It is NOT available to this framework, whose derived EFE gives
  the Amendment-2 orientation-averaged gamma_v = 1.0799 with NO theta freedom at all.
  Framework (derived, Amendment 2) 1.0799 vs frozen MG 1.137: separation 0.0571 = 3.0 sigma.
  [OK] for THIS framework the MI-MG separation is only 3.0 sigma -- so section 1.5's forecast that MI-vs-MG is likely UNDECIDABLE in DR4 stands unamended, exactly as Amendment 2 (g) said. No frozen number moves as a result of this script

====================================================================================================
S6. Does it cost anything on the RAR? No -- by his normalisation, and checked
====================================================================================================
  Milgrom fixes theta(1) = 1 precisely so that, verbatim, 'it is mu(a_ex/a0) that enters the
  rotation-curve analysis'. theta modifies the EXTERNAL-field term only (Eq. 34's second
  piece), so a system whose internal acceleration dominates is untouched. Structural check:
   y = g_bar/a0  nu (theta-free)   nu with theta(0)=3 on a_ex=0    ratio
          0.010        10.049876                      10.049876 1.000000
          0.100         3.316625                       3.316625 1.000000
          1.000         1.414214                       1.414214 1.000000
         10.000         1.048809                       1.048809 1.000000
        100.000         1.004988                       1.004988 1.000000
  [OK] for an ISOLATED system the theta term is multiplied by a_ex = 0 and drops out identically for any theta(0), so the SPARC RAR fit (0.108 dex) is untouched -- this is not a tuning, it is what theta(1)=1 was chosen to guarantee

====================================================================================================
S7. WHAT ACTUALLY SURVIVES: theta separates this framework from the MI family
====================================================================================================
  The interesting residue is a DISTINCTION, not a rescue. Milgrom's MI models carry a free
  function theta with theta(0) of order a few; this framework's closure pins theta = 1 by two
  independent theorems. So the two make DIFFERENT wide-binary predictions, and that is a
  falsifiability virtue for the framework: fewer free functions, a more rigid prediction.
  model                                                   gamma_v
  this framework (derived, quadrature, no theta)             1.0799
  Milgrom MI, theta(y)=2/(1+y^2)                    1.0578-1.0823
  Milgrom MI, theta(y)=e^(1-y)                      1.0422-1.0601
  Milgrom MI, theta(y)=e^((1-y)/q), q=1/1.1         1.0382-1.0542

  Separation from the theta(0)=2 case: 0.0104 in gamma_v = 0.5 sigma at the frozen DR4 error model.
  [OK] framework-vs-Milgrom-MI separates by only 0.5 sigma, so DR4 cannot distinguish them either -- the distinction is real in principle and NOT a near-term test. Reported as such rather than promoted to a front

====================================================================================================
VERDICT
====================================================================================================
  1. THE DOOR DOES NOT DO WHAT IT WAS OPENED FOR. Milgrom's frequency-ratio construction
     does NOT close the alpha=1 planetary liability, and the failure is structural rather
     than quantitative: theta enters additively against a_in, Earth has a_in/a_ex = 3.3e+07,
     and the required theta(0) ~ 4.3e+10 is ten orders above his own examples. Deeper:
     the a0/2 anomaly is a single-frequency effect and a ratio construction has no purchase
     on a one-frequency trajectory. So the fifth constant omega_c is NOT shown unnecessary --
     it was doing a job ratios structurally cannot do. That hypothesis is CLOSED.
  2. AND THIS FRAMEWORK CANNOT USE IT EVEN WHERE IT WOULD HELP. In the wide-binary regime
     theta(0) of a few WOULD pull gamma_v from ~1.14 to 1.04-1.08, below the frozen MI point
     target 1.09. A first pass of this script concluded an amendment was owed on that basis.
     THAT IS WITHDRAWN. Two committed results, both predating this script, forbid it: the
     kernel is EXACTLY unimodular on the oscillatory branch so it cannot source theta's
     y-dependence (sympy), and Theorem B forces the closure's argument to |a_total|^2 --
     QUADRATURE with a vector cross term, not Milgrom's linear a_in + theta a_ex. Amendment 2
     to the DR4 pre-registration already says exactly this and was right. NO FROZEN NUMBER
     MOVES. Recording the near-miss because amending a frozen target on a basis the theory
     cannot use would have been a worse error than any wrong number in this file.
  3. SO MI-vs-MG STAYS UNDECIDABLE HERE. The 3.7-4.9 sigma separation theta would buy is real
     but belongs to Milgrom's general MI models. This framework's derived EFE gives 1.0799
     with no theta freedom, i.e. 3.0 sigma from the MG target -- section 1.5's forecast stands
     unamended, as does Amendment 2 (g).
  4. WHAT SURVIVES IS A DISTINCTION, NOT A RESCUE, and it cuts in the framework's favour on
     falsifiability: Milgrom's MI carries a free theta, this framework pins theta = 1 by two
     theorems. Fewer free functions, a more rigid wide-binary prediction. But the two
     predictions separate by under 3 sigma at the frozen DR4 error model, so this is a real
     distinction and NOT a near-term test. Not promoted to a front.
  5. NET: the door is CLOSED, on three independent grounds -- it cannot reach the planets
     (a_in/a_ex = 3.3e7), the kernel cannot supply theta, and Theorem B forbids the linear
     combination it needs. omega_c is NOT shown unnecessary. alpha=1 stands exactly where it
     did: exactness versus the planets, and only one survives.
  [OK] verdict recorded: door CLOSED on three independent grounds; NO frozen number moves; the near-miss amendment is recorded as withdrawn
====================================================================================================
